## Must fix before publish

1. **Medication/BMI eligibility is too absolute**

> Medical weight management options including GLP-1s may be suitable if you have a BMI of 30 or higher.

> Non-surgical medical weight management may be worth discussing from a BMI of 27 or above, particularly where weight-related health conditions are present. Medication suitability depends on the specific medicine, your medical history and current Australian prescribing guidance, while surgical Medicare eligibility is assessed separately.

Why it matters: The specific BMI 30+ suitability claim is too direct and should be framed as an assessment conversation, especially where medication suitability and surgical Medicare eligibility are different questions.

2. **The surgery definition creates an unbalanced success claim**

> These are all well established procedures with long track records of weight loss success in Australia.

> These procedures have been used in Australia for many years, but the likely benefits, risks and suitability vary by person and should be discussed with your surgeon.

Why it matters: "Long track records of weight loss success" turns an explanatory definition into a broad promotional outcome claim.

3. **The dietitian FAQ makes an unsupported clinical outcome claim**

> Patients who engage with dietitian support consistently achieve better outcomes, and because our dietitians are in-house, they can work closely with your surgeon throughout your care.

> Dietitian support can help you prepare for treatment, protect your nutrition and build habits that support long-term weight management; because our dietitians are in-house, they can work closely with your surgeon throughout your care.

Why it matters: The rewrite explains the role of dietitian support without making a comparative clinical outcome claim.

4. **Residual GLP-1, safety, outcome and insurance claims remain** (G1; draft L98, L150, L175-180, L186-192, L211-216, L243-245, L286-294, L134-139)

   a. **Dietitian research claim — draft L148-151**

   > Dedicated Accredited Practising Dietitians ensure nutritional support is consistent and central to your care, which research consistently identifies as a major factor in lasting results.

   > Accredited Practising Dietitians provide nutrition support as part of the care plan and can work closely with your surgeon throughout your care.

   b. **Secondary GLP-1 mention — draft L175-176**

   > GLP-1 medications can have side effects that need monitoring. Very low calorie diets require supervision to be done safely.

   > Some medicines may have side effects that need monitoring. Very low calorie diets require clinical supervision.

   c. **Comparative safety claim — draft L178-180**

   > But for many patients, the documented health risks of not treating obesity are greater than the risk of treatment.

   > The health risks of untreated obesity and the risks of treatment are different for every person; your clinician can help you weigh both as part of an individual assessment.

   d. **Secondary GLP-1 mention — draft L186-188**

   > GLP-1 medications are typically taken long-term and can cause nausea, digestive side effects, and muscle loss, and so are not suitable for everyone.

   > Some medicines may cause nausea, digestive symptoms or other side effects; a clinician should assess whether they are appropriate for you.

   e. **Effectiveness claim — draft L190-192**

   > Very low calorie diets are an effective short-term tool but require clinical supervision and are not a viable long-term eating pattern.

   > Very low calorie diets may be used for some patients under clinical supervision, but are not designed as a permanent eating pattern.

   f. **Insurance-coverage claim — draft L211-216**

   > For patients with appropriate private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates apply when eligibility criteria are met.

   > Private health cover and any Medicare rebates may affect out-of-pocket costs, but what applies depends on your policy, waiting periods, hospital excess, current MBS criteria and the fees for your care; discuss the applicable costs at consultation.

   g. **Secondary GLP-1 mention — draft L243-245**

   > Medical weight management uses non-surgical tools such as GLP-1 medications and structured dietary programs to support weight loss and improve related health conditions.

   > Medical weight management uses non-surgical tools such as prescribed medicines and structured dietary programs; suitability and likely effects depend on your medical history and clinician assessment.

   h. **Outcome-certainty claim — draft L286-294**

   > Weight loss surgery and medical weight management can both produce significant, lasting results, but no pathway is entirely passive. Long-term success depends on the habits and support built around treatment, and some weight regain over time *is* possible if those aren't maintained. Our team stays involved well beyond your initial treatment, adjusting your care as your needs change. The goal is sustainable results over the long term, not just weight loss in the short term.

   > Weight loss surgery and medical weight management may help some people, but results vary and weight regain can occur. Follow-up focuses on long-term weight management, with the plan adjusted as your needs change.

   i. **Results-protection claim — draft L95-98**

   > Commitment to long-term dietary and lifestyle changes, supplements where needed, and ongoing follow-up: understanding that surgery is your starting point, and aftercare is what protects your results

   > Commitment to long-term dietary and lifestyle changes, supplements where needed, and ongoing follow-up: surgery is a starting point, and aftercare is part of ongoing weight-management support.

   j. **Long-term follow-up outcome wording — draft L134-139**

   > Weight management is an ongoing process. Our team will continue to review your progress, adapt your plan as your needs change, and support sustainable results well beyond your initial treatment.

   > Weight management is ongoing. Our team will review your progress, adapt your plan as your needs change, and provide follow-up after treatment.

   The GLP-1 references at draft L69 and L259-262 are already covered by Minor 2 and Must fix 3. Keep only active-ingredient/class names such as semaglutide, liraglutide or GLP-1 if a medicine reference is necessary; never add brand names. **Pending decision:** confirm the current TGA/legal advertising position for GLP-1 and medical-weight-management copy before publication.

5. **The lower-BMI eligibility bullets are not MBS-accurate** (G2; draft L80-90, including the known residual at draft L88)

   > BMI 40+: surgery is generally an appropriate option to discuss, regardless of other conditions
   >
   > BMI 35–40 and a related condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint disease, or gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD): surgery is commonly considered
   >
   > BMI 30–35: may be considered in select cases, often where diabetes is difficult to manage with medication alone; assessed on an individual basis

   > Eligibility is assessed individually. For a Medicare-listed bariatric surgery pathway, the relevant MBS thresholds are BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity; a BMI below 35 does not meet that MBS threshold. Your surgeon must assess the clinical options, risks and suitability for your circumstances.

   This replaces the implication that BMI 30-35 is a surgical pathway and avoids treating “may be considered” as eligibility.

