--- brief ---
# Content Brief: Weight Loss Surgery Perth
**URL:** /weight-loss-surgery-perth
**Primary keyword:** weight loss surgery perth
**Search intent:** informational / commercial
**Target word count:** 1,100–1,400 words (matches brief; SERP is dominated by short-to-mid commercial clinic homepages, so the brief's depth is enough to compete while the spectrum-of-care breadth differentiates)

## Page purpose
This is the broadest, highest-traffic page on the site — it captures people who are at the very start of their research and are not yet attached to a specific procedure. Its job is to introduce the full spectrum of care (medical management through to surgery), make the reader feel seen and un-judged, and position OSWA as the team to help them work out what is right for them.

## Keyword Intelligence

### Primary Keyword
- Keyword: weight loss surgery perth
- AU Monthly Volume: 250
- Keyword Difficulty: 45/100
- CPC (AU): ~$1.3

### Secondary Keywords (target naturally in body copy)
| Keyword | AU Vol/mo | KD |
|---|---|---|
| bariatric surgery perth | 150 | 52 |
| perth weight loss surgery | 100 | 46 |
| weight loss surgery perth cost | 90 | 0 |
| gastric bypass perth | 200 | 42 |
| gastric band | 500 | 8 |
| weight loss clinic perth | 100 | 2 |
| weight loss doctor perth | 30 | 4 |
| using super for weight loss surgery perth | 30 | 0 |

### Semantic Terms to Include
Comma-separated list of LSI/semantic terms the writer must naturally weave into the copy. These are not keyword-stuff targets — they are terms that signal topical depth to Google.

bariatric surgery, gastric sleeve, sleeve gastrectomy, gastric bypass, adjustable gastric band, lap band, obesity as a medical condition, BMI, GLP-1 medication, very low calorie diet, dietitian support, type 2 diabetes, Medicare rebate, private health cover, endoscopic option, weight regain, Booragoon, Mandurah

### SERP Landscape (AU)
The SERP is almost entirely commercial: individual Perth bariatric surgeon and clinic homepages (Dr Siva Gounder / perthweightlosssurgery.com.au, perthbariatricsurgery.com.au / Dr Andrew Kiyingi, Advance Surgical / Jon Armstrong, Aurora Bariatrics / Dr Ruth Blackham, Dr Kevin Dolan, Perth Surgical & Bariatrics, Evolve Bariatrics). Domain ratings are low across the board (DR 1-26), so this is a winnable, weakly-defended SERP. Almost every result is a surgeon-led homepage selling a procedure menu — not informational guides. People Also Ask boxes surface clear informational intent: "How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?", "Is weight loss surgery covered by Medicare in Australia?", and "What BMI is used to assess weight loss surgery?". Intent is mixed commercial + informational, which fits the brief's positioning of this as the broad top-of-funnel page.

Top 5 ranking pages:
| # | Domain | Page Title |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | perthweightlosssurgery.com.au | Perth Weight Loss Surgery — Dr Siva Gounder |
| 2 | perthbariatricsurgery.com.au | Perth Bariatric Surgery — Dr Andrew Kiyingi |
| 3 | advancesurgical.com.au | Weight Loss Surgery Perth — Advance Surgical (Jon Armstrong) |
| 4 | aurorabariatrics.com.au | Aurora Bariatrics — Dr Ruth Blackham |
| 5 | obesitysurgerywa.com.au | Weight Loss Surgery Perth — Obesity Surgery WA (OSWA, DR 11) |

### Content Gap Opportunity
Every top result is a surgeon homepage that funnels straight to surgery — none present medical management (GLP-1 medication, VLCD, dietitian support) as an equal pathway. That is OSWA's biggest differentiation opportunity and aligns exactly with the brief's mandate to frame obesity as a medical condition and not over-index on surgery. Second gap: the PAA boxes show high demand for cost, Medicare coverage, and BMI/eligibility answers, yet competitor homepages bury or omit these — directly addressing how medical management is assessed from BMI 27+, how Medicare rebates and MBS thresholds work, and how much treatment may cost / whether superannuation access can be explored in plain language (the brief already mandates an eligibility section, cost paragraph, and FAQs) will capture featured-snippet and PAA real estate competitors leave open. Third gap: low-DR competitors mean strong topical depth, empathetic non-judgemental framing, and a team-based (dedicated APD dietitian team, physicians + surgeons, 2 clinics) angle can outrank them without heavy backlinks. Note OSWA (obesitysurgerywa.com.au) currently sits at position 5 with DR 11, so this page is an existing-asset improvement play, not a cold start.

### Recommended Word Count
1,100-1,400 words (matches brief; SERP is dominated by short-to-mid commercial clinic homepages, so the brief's depth is enough to compete while the spectrum-of-care breadth differentiates).

## Section guidance

### Hero
- Headline direction: Frame the page as covering more than just surgery — it is about finding the right starting point. The headline should signal expertise and approachability in the same breath.
- Subheadline: 2–3 sentences. Acknowledge the emotional reality of years of trying (without framing the reader as a failure). Introduce OSWA as a team — surgeons, physicians, and dietitians — not just a surgical service. Make clear the team meets the reader where they are, including if they are not yet ready for surgery.

### Stats bar (4 stats)
Use these factual anchors. Keep the BMI stat explicitly assessment-only; it is not a surgical MBS threshold:

| Label | Value | Note |
|---|---|---|
| Dietitians on the team | APD | Accredited Practising Dietitians; ongoing support is part of the plan, not an afterthought |
| Perth clinics | 2 | Booragoon and Mandurah |
| Decades of experience | 20+ | Bariatric surgery in Perth |
| Medical-team assessment from | BMI 27+ | Individual assessment; not a surgical MBS threshold |

### What is weight loss surgery — and why it is only part of the picture
- Section title: "What we mean by weight loss surgery, and why it is only part of the picture"
- ~4 paragraphs (this page warrants a slightly longer treatment given its breadth)
- Para 1: Define weight loss surgery / bariatric surgery in plain terms — procedures that change stomach size or function to help the patient eat less and feel full sooner. Name the most common procedures: gastric sleeve, gastric bypass, adjustable gastric band. State that these procedures have been used in Australia for many years, while likely benefits, risks and suitability vary by person.
- Para 2: Establish clearly that surgery is not the only road, and not always the first. Explain that obesity is a medical condition, not a willpower failure — this framing matters because it shapes what good treatment looks like. The right answer might be medication, a structured very low calorie diet, dietitian support, an endoscopic option, or surgery — often in combination over time.
- Para 3: Introduce the team composition — not just surgeons; also physicians who manage the medical side of weight, and specialist dietitians whose support is central to the care plan. Whoever the patient sees first, they are stepping into a team that can move them along the spectrum of care as their needs change.
- Para 4: State the aim of the page plainly — to help the reader understand the full range of options so they can make an informed decision, rather than feeling pushed toward any single one.
- Closing comparison note (below paragraphs): Give the reader a quick mental model of the spectrum: medical management (GLP-1 medication, very low calorie diet, dietitian support) at one end, surgical procedures (sleeve, bypass, band) at the other. Acknowledge that many people move between them over time, and there is no single correct starting point.

### Eligibility section
- BMI summary to anchor the section: "You do not need a very high BMI to start a conversation. A BMI of 27 or above, particularly where a weight-related health condition is present, is a reason to request an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options. For the Medicare-listed surgical pathway, current MBS thresholds refer to BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity. We will help you understand what needs to be assessed."
- Write each of the following points — keep them accessible and non-clinical:
  - BMI around 27+ with a condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea: invite an individual medical assessment for non-surgical options
  - Current MBS surgical thresholds: BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity; clinical suitability is assessed individually
  - Higher BMI with prior attempts at weight loss that did not hold: surgical and non-surgical options can be assessed together
  - Readiness to engage with ongoing support (dietitian appointments) as part of any pathway
  - Age, general health, previous surgery, and current medications are all considered at assessment
  - If the reader is unsure what can be assessed: that uncertainty is exactly what an initial consultation is for — explain the options without promising eligibility

### How it works (5 steps)
Keep steps in this order — 1–2 sentences covering the content described:

1. **An honest first consultation** — the team listens to history, prior attempts, health, and what the patient actually wants; no judgement; this conversation shapes everything that follows
2. **Assessment by the right team member** — depending on the situation, this may be a physician, surgeon, or dietitian first; patients are matched to the part of the team that fits where they are starting from
3. **A pathway proposed, not prescribed** — the team lays out realistic options from medical management through to surgery, explains likely benefits and trade-offs of each; the patient decides together with the team
4. **Treatment, with support built in** — whether the plan involves medication, a very low calorie diet, an endoscopic option, or an operation, the dietitians and clinical team stay involved throughout
5. **Long-term follow-up** — weight management is ongoing; the team reviews progress, adjusts the plan as needs change, and helps sustain results over time

### Benefits section
- Section title: "Why a team-based approach tends to work"
- 6 bullets, one idea each:
  1. Assessment across the full spectrum of care means the patient is not limited to a single treatment
  2. Dedicated dietitians mean nutritional support is consistent and central to the care plan and long-term follow-up
  3. Physicians can manage related conditions such as type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure alongside weight
  4. If the patient starts with medical management and later decides surgery is right, the transition happens within the same team that already knows their history
  5. Both Perth clinic locations (Booragoon and Mandurah) keep appointments and aftercare close to home
  6. Many people experience improvements in weight-related conditions and day-to-day quality of life — individual results vary and no specific outcome can be promised

### Risks — honest section
- Section title: "Being honest about the trade-offs"
- Intro paragraph: Acknowledge that every option on the spectrum carries its own considerations. Frame this as a genuine service to the reader — the team will not pretend otherwise. Medications have side effects. Very low calorie diets need supervision. Surgery is a significant step with real risks and recovery. The clinician should explain how these considerations relate to the reader's medical situation before any commitment.
- Risk bullets to address (one sentence each):
  - GLP-1 medications can cause nausea, digestive symptoms and other side effects; not suitable for everyone
  - Very low calorie diets require supervision and are not designed as a permanent eating pattern
  - Surgical procedures carry risks including bleeding, infection, leaks, blood clots and anaesthetic reactions
  - After surgery, lifelong changes to eating and, in some cases, vitamin supplementation are needed
  - No pathway works without ongoing effort; weight regain is possible if the plan is not maintained
  - The best option for someone else may not be right for this reader — individual assessment matters

### Cost paragraph
Cover: cost depends entirely on which pathway is chosen — medical management and dietitian support look very different in cost to surgery; within surgery the procedure type matters too; private health cover, Medicare rebates, and in some cases superannuation access can all play a part; any applicable estimate or range should be discussed at consultation for the specific situation. Do not quote specific dollar figures.

### Conversion CTA headline
Direction: The reader on this page is at an earlier stage than on the procedure-specific pages — they are still working out where to start. The CTA headline should validate that uncertainty as the right reason to reach out, not an obstacle.
Reference tone: "Not sure where to start? That is exactly the right reason to talk to us."

### FAQs (12 questions)
The page must answer each of the following:

1. I am not sure I want surgery — can I still come and see you?
2. What is the difference between medical management and surgery?
3. Do I really need to see a dietitian, or can I just have the procedure?
4. Where are your Perth clinics, and where is surgery performed?
5. How do I know which pathway is right for me?
6. Will my weight loss be permanent?
7. Do I need a GP referral to be seen?
8. How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?
9. What is the 30/30 eating rule after surgery?
10. Who may not meet the Medicare pathway for weight loss surgery?
11. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery in Australia?
12. What are the 10-year outcomes after weight loss surgery?

---

## Tone and compliance notes
- Medical advertising: no guarantees, use phrases like "clinical averages", "results vary", "your clinician will advise", "many people experience"
- AHPRA/TGA: no testimonials, no before/after claims, no guaranteed outcomes
- MBS surgical threshold: state BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity as assessment guidance; BMI below 35 does not meet that MBS threshold. Do not present a lower-BMI statement as surgical eligibility.
- GLP-1/medical-weight-management advertising policy is pending: use active-ingredient/class names only (semaglutide, liraglutide or GLP-1), never brand names, and verify the final TGA/legal position before publication
- Voice: plain-language, empathetic, direct; avoid hype; this page is the broadest entry point and the reader may be at their most uncertain and guarded
- Reader: a Perth adult at the beginning of their research, possibly BMI 27–35, may not know the word "bariatric", may feel shame or exhaustion — the page must normalise the conversation before it can sell it
- This page should do the most to establish "obesity as a medical condition, not a willpower failure" — that framing is the emotional foundation the rest of the site is built on
- Do not over-index on surgery; the medical management pathway is genuinely presented as an equal option on this page

