content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-cost-perth.md:112:**Opening paragraph:** Identify the most common source of financial surprise — unexpected out-of-pocket gaps — and explain they usually arise from not checking private health cover, being unaware of waiting periods, or comparing only the surgeon's fee while overlooking the hospital and anaesthetist. Framing: knowing these in advance is the protection.
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-cost-perth.md:116:- Inadequate or recently upgraded hospital cover can mean a waiting period applies or limited benefit is paid
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-cost-perth.md:150:2. Why are there so many separate fees? (Surgeon, anaesthetist, hospital each bill independently; dietitian and physician assessment also billed separately; the practice brings these together in one estimate)
content-briefs/gastric-sleeve-cost-perth.md:152:4. Will my private health insurance pay for it? (Requires appropriate hospital cover including bariatric surgery item numbers; most funds apply a 12-month waiting period; the practice helps confirm what the fund will contribute before the patient commits)
content-briefs/revision-bariatric-surgery.md:38:The AU SERP is dominated almost entirely by surgeon- and clinic-owned procedure pages (Aurora Bariatrics, Adelaide Bariatric Centre, Sydney Bariatric Clinic, Gold Coast Weight Loss, Dr Govind Krishna, Dr Scott Whiting, Dr Lepage, Hunter Weight Loss, Upper GI West) plus private hospital service pages (Healthscope, Norwest Private). A handful of large US informational/authority pages also appear (Cleveland Clinic, Penn Medicine, Loyola Medicine) competing on the head term for definitional/"who qualifies" intent. There are no dedicated cost calculators and only one YouTube video (Endoscopic Gastric Revision). Intent is mixed commercial + informational: searchers want to know what revision is, whether they qualify, and to find a surgeon who handles complex second operations. Pages are structured around procedure types (sleeve-to-bypass, band conversion, bypass revision), candidacy/eligibility, the assessment process, and risk.
content-briefs/revision-bariatric-surgery.md:71:| Where | SJOG Murdoch & Subiaco | Performed at St John of God private hospitals |
content-briefs/revision-bariatric-surgery.md:115:  - Increased risk of leaks at surgical connections — careful monitoring in hospital
content-briefs/revision-bariatric-surgery.md:121:Cover: revision is often more involved than a first procedure, which can affect total cost. Final figure depends on private health cover, surgeon and anaesthetist fees, and hospital stay. Medicare and most private health funds with appropriate cover contribute toward eligible bariatric surgery; some patients access superannuation early release for out-of-pocket costs. Do not quote a single figure. State that every patient receives a clear, itemised written estimate before any decision is made.
