content-briefs/surgeons.md:50:Opportunity 1: No ranking competitor leads with a surgeon who has personally undergone bariatric surgery — Dr Chandraratna's lived experience is a genuine, AHPRA-compliant differentiator no other Perth result can claim. Opportunity 2: Most competitors are single-surgeon sites; OSWA can own the 'team and procedure-matching' angle (two FRACS surgeons + in-house physicians/dietitians = continuity of care) which the field largely ignores. Opportunity 3: The PAA shows clear demand for cost and 'how to choose' guidance, yet ranking pages are thin on neutral consumer guidance — the comparison-note section (what actually makes a good bariatric surgeon: FRACS, accredited hospital privileges, aftercare quality) fills a real gap and earns the PAA real estate. Opportunity 4: Few competitors address Medicare item numbers, private cover gap payments, or super access factually — covering these honestly without dollar figures builds trust competitors don't. Note: low overall DR across the SERP means strong on-page credibility (named surgeons, FRACS, named hospitals, Bariatric Surgery Registry) plus FAQ schema is likely enough to break into the top half.
content-briefs/surgeons.md:66:2. **Hospitals / 2** — St John of God Murdoch and St John of God Subiaco
content-briefs/surgeons.md:73:Introduce him fully: FRACS credential, University of Notre Dame lectureship, operating hospitals (St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco). Include the personal detail — he has been through bariatric surgery himself — and frame it as something that informs how he treats patients, not as a selling point. Keep this factual and grounded.
content-briefs/surgeons.md:82:A short, practical paragraph guiding readers who are comparing surgeons. Tell them what actually matters when assessing bariatric surgeons: FRACS fellowship, accredited hospital privileges, quality of the aftercare team, and whether the surgeon takes time to understand individual circumstances. This is genuinely useful consumer guidance, not a competitive put-down.
content-briefs/surgeons.md:88:- Both surgeons operate at accredited private hospitals
content-briefs/surgeons.md:101:5. **Hospital Recovery** — Recovery in hospital (typically a few nights) with nursing and dietitian support before discharge
content-briefs/surgeons.md:108:- Operating at St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco (accredited hospitals with full theatre and ICU backup)
content-briefs/surgeons.md:126:Explain that costs depend on the procedure chosen, private health cover level, and individual circumstances. Note that most bariatric procedures attract a Medicare item number and that patients with appropriate private hospital cover typically pay a gap rather than the full fee. Mention that early superannuation access is an option some patients use. Emphasise that a written, itemised estimate is provided before any commitment is made — no surprises. Do not quote specific dollar figures on this page.
content-briefs/surgeons.md:132:Cover: lead surgeon and founder role, FRACS fellowship, Notre Dame lectureship, operating hospitals, and personal bariatric surgery experience and what it means for his patient approach. Three to four sentences.
content-briefs/surgeons.md:146:1. What makes a good bariatric surgeon in Perth? (FRACS, hospital accreditation, aftercare team, time taken with patients)
content-briefs/surgeons.md:150:5. Which hospitals do the surgeons operate at? (St John of God Murdoch and Subiaco; consulting at Booragoon and Mandurah)
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth.md:35:gastric sleeve, gastric bypass, gastric banding, sleeve gastrectomy, bariatric surgery, out-of-pocket cost, MBS item 31575, MBS item 31572, Medicare rebate, private health insurance, 12-month waiting period, gold-tier hospital cover, surgeon's fee, anaesthetist fee, hospital fee, compassionate release of super, BMI 35, payment plan, written estimate
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth.md:53:3. **Answer the misunderstandings honestly** — the 12-month waiting period as a cost-saving decision, and Medicare NOT covering the hospital admission, are common misunderstandings surfaced in PAA and Reddit but poorly answered by clinic pages; direct, honest coverage wins featured-snippet and AI-citation potential.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth.md:83:Name all the components: surgeon's fee, anaesthetist's fee, assistant surgeon's fee (where applicable), and the hospital fee. Note that there can also be costs for the dietitian, follow-up appointments and pre-operative tests. Each is billed separately — this is why the headline number can feel confusing.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth.md:89:Explain that the biggest determinant of what the patient actually pays is whether they have appropriate private health insurance and whether the waiting period has been served. With the right cover, the hospital fee is largely covered and out-of-pocket drops considerably. Without it, the hospital component becomes a significant self-funded cost.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth.md:92:Short, honest note: every range on this page uses "approximately" or "around" deliberately. Costs move with the procedure, the surgeon's assessment, the insurer's policy and the hospital. The practice does not ask patients to make decisions on a ballpark — a written estimate comes first.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth.md:97:- **Private health cover — what to check:** Patients generally need gold-tier or equivalent hospital cover that explicitly includes the obesity surgery item numbers. Identify the key Medicare item numbers: 31575 (sleeve) and 31572 (bypass). Stress the difference between a general "weight management" category and the specific item numbers — check the specific items.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth.md:99:- **Medicare and the hospital admission:** Medicare provides a rebate on surgical and consultation fees for eligible patients but does NOT cover the hospital admission for elective bariatric surgery. That cost is either covered by private health or paid out-of-pocket.
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth.md:108:3. **Written, itemised estimate** — Before any commitment, a personalised estimate lists the surgeon, anaesthetist, assistant and hospital fees, what the insurer is expected to cover, and the likely out-of-pocket; no surprises is the practice standard
content-briefs/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth.md:127:- Assuming all private health policies cover obesity surgery — many do not; some cover consultations but not the hospital item numbers; always check 31575 and 31572 specifically

