+
+   > **8. How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?**
+   > Costs depend on the pathway, procedure, hospital, private health cover, waiting periods, hospital excess, anaesthetist and other fees. Any applicable estimate or range should be discussed at consultation after your team understands your situation; no fixed cost applies to everyone. Confirm any Medicare rebate or private-cover contribution for your circumstances.
+   >
+   > **9. What is the 30/30 eating rule after surgery?**
+   > A commonly used 30/30 approach is to avoid drinking for about 30 minutes before a meal and wait about 30 minutes after eating before drinking again. Your dietitian or surgeon will confirm whether this applies at your stage of recovery and give you the relevant eating and drinking plan.
+   >
+   > **10. Who may not meet the Medicare pathway for weight loss surgery?**
+   > The MBS thresholds are BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity. A person below BMI 35 does not meet that MBS threshold; a person at BMI 35-39.9 needs the relevant major co-morbidity. Clinical suitability also depends on medical history, surgical risk, readiness for follow-up and individual clinician assessment.
+   >
+   > **11. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery in Australia?**
+   > Medicare may provide rebates for eligible specialist consultations and MBS-listed bariatric surgery services when current criteria are met. The relevant procedure items are 31575 for sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 for Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and 31581 for BPD/duodenal switch. Private health cover, waiting periods, hospital excess and anaesthetist fees can affect out-of-pocket costs, so confirm what applies before deciding.
+   >
+   > **12. What are the 10-year outcomes after weight loss surgery?**
+   > Ten-year outcomes vary by procedure, health history, follow-up and other factors. Weight regain or changes in related conditions can occur, so no permanent result should be promised. Ask your surgeon to explain the long-term evidence, trade-offs and follow-up relevant to the procedure being considered.
