docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-328-  > **Gastric bypass (Roux-en-Y)**: restricts food intake and changes how nutrients are absorbed. Typically results in greater weight loss than a sleeve, often ~70–80% of excess weight, though individual results will vary. Frequently preferred for type 2 diabetes due to its effect on blood sugar. Requires lifelong vitamin and mineral monitoring and supplementation.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-329-  >
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-330-  > **Mini gastric bypass (one anastomosis bypass)**: a simplified variation of the Roux-en-Y bypass that creates a single connection between the stomach and small intestine, rather than two. The result is a shorter, less complex operation with a similar weight loss outcome for most patients, though with a slightly different risk profile. Your surgeon can advise whether it's a suitable option for your circumstances.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-331-  >
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-332-  > **Gastric band (adjustable band)**: a reversible procedure in which an inflatable silicone band is placed around the upper stomach, creating a small pouch that slows eating and promotes fullness. No cutting or stapling is involved and the band can be adjusted or removed. It generally produces the least weight loss of the surgical options and requires ongoing follow-up for adjustments.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-333-  >
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-334-  > **Revision surgery**: for patients who have had a previous bariatric procedure and require a conversion, a repair, or further intervention (for example, a band that is no longer working well or causing complications). Revision surgery is generally more complex than a primary procedure, and risks and benefits will be assessed individually with your surgeon.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md-335-  >
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/bariatric-surgery-perth.md:336:  > **Medical weight management**: non-surgical weight loss treatment pathways including GLP-1 medications (such as semaglutide) and structured dietitian-led programs. Suits people who do not want surgery, are not yet candidates, or who would like specialist medical support before or instead of proceeding with a surgical procedure.
